Write First C Program to Call Windows APIs
Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Write a C program that calls Windows API functions on Linux using Wine
Compile Windows-compatible C code using MinGW cross-compiler
Execute Windows programs in a Linux environment
Understand the basics of Windows API programming
Prerequisites
Basic knowledge of C programming language
Familiarity with Linux command line
Understanding of compilation process
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required software during the lab.

Task 1: Write C Code to Call the MessageBox API
Step 1.1: Install Required Tools
Install Wine and MinGW cross-compiler:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y wine64 gcc-mingw-w64 nano
Configure Wine:

winecfg
Select Windows 10 from the dropdown and click OK.

Step 1.2: Create the C Program
Create a new directory and navigate to it:

mkdir windows-api-lab
cd windows-api-lab
Create the C source file:

nano messagebox.c
Add the following code:

#include <windows.h>

int WINAPI WinMain(HINSTANCE hInstance, HINSTANCE hPrevInstance, 
                   LPSTR lpCmdLine, int nCmdShow) {
    
    MessageBox(NULL, 
               "Hello from Windows API!\nThis program runs on Linux using Wine.", 
               "My First Windows API Program", 
               MB_OK | MB_ICONINFORMATION);
    
    MessageBox(NULL, 
               "Do you want to see another message?", 
               "Question", 
               MB_YESNO | MB_ICONQUESTION);
    
    return 0;
}
Save and exit (Ctrl+X, then Y, then Enter).

Step 1.3: Verify the Code
Display the created file to confirm:

cat messagebox.c
Task 2: Compile and Execute the Program
Step 2.1: Compile the Program
Compile using MinGW cross-compiler:

x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o messagebox.exe messagebox.c -luser32 -lgdi32
Verify the executable was created:

ls -la messagebox.exe
Step 2.2: Execute the Program
Run the Windows executable using Wine:

wine messagebox.exe
You should see two message boxes appear:

An information dialog with "Hello from Windows API!"
A question dialog asking if you want to see another message
Step 2.3: Create an Advanced Example
Create a more complex Windows API program:

nano advanced_api.c
Add the following code:

#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int WINAPI WinMain(HINSTANCE hInstance, HINSTANCE hPrevInstance, 
                   LPSTR lpCmdLine, int nCmdShow) {
    
    // Get system information
    SYSTEM_INFO sysInfo;
    GetSystemInfo(&sysInfo);
    
    char buffer[256];
    sprintf(buffer, "System Information:\n\nProcessor Architecture: %d\nNumber of Processors: %d\nPage Size: %d bytes", 
            sysInfo.wProcessorArchitecture, 
            sysInfo.dwNumberOfProcessors, 
            sysInfo.dwPageSize);
    
    MessageBox(NULL, buffer, "System Info via Windows API", MB_OK | MB_ICONINFORMATION);
    
    // Get Windows directory
    char winDir[MAX_PATH];
    GetWindowsDirectory(winDir, MAX_PATH);
    
    sprintf(buffer, "Windows Directory: %s", winDir);
    MessageBox(NULL, buffer, "Windows Directory", MB_OK | MB_ICONINFORMATION);
    
    return 0;
}
Save and exit.

Step 2.4: Compile and Run Advanced Example
Compile the advanced program:

x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o advanced_api.exe advanced_api.c -luser32 -lkernel32
Execute the program:

wine advanced_api.exe
Step 2.5: Verify Installation and Execution
Check Wine processes:

ps aux | grep wine
List created executables:

ls -la *.exe
Troubleshooting
If Wine fails to start:

export DISPLAY=:0
wine --version
If compilation fails:

x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc --version
dpkg -l | grep mingw
If message boxes don't appear:

winecfg
# Ensure Graphics tab shows proper display settings
Verification
Confirm successful completion by running:

echo "Lab completed successfully!"
echo "Created executables:"
ls -la *.exe
echo "Wine version:"
wine --version
Conclusion
You have successfully created and executed C programs that call Windows APIs on a Linux system. This lab demonstrated cross-platform development using MinGW for compilation and Wine for execution. You learned to use essential Windows APIs like MessageBox, GetSystemInfo, and GetWindowsDirectory. These skills are valuable for developing Windows applications in cross-platform environments and understanding how Windows APIs function at a low level.

The ability to develop Windows programs on Linux systems is crucial for developers working in mixed environments and provides flexibility in development workflows without requiring Windows-specific hardware or virtual machines.
